# Drivebit Clients - Cursor Rules

## Agent Behavior
- **Do not tell the user what to do** — you do the work yourself to achieve the result. Prefer making changes, running commands, and iterating until the goal is met instead of giving step-by-step instructions for the user to follow.

## Project Overview
This is a Kotlin Multiplatform project for Drivebit mobile clients targeting Android, iOS, and Web platforms.

## Code Style & Conventions
- Use Kotlin coding conventions
- Follow Material Design guidelines for Android
- Follow iOS Human Interface Guidelines for iOS
- **Write UI maximally in Compose** - prefer Compose Multiplatform for all shared and platform UI
- **Reuse existing components** - before adding new UI, search the codebase for existing components (buttons, inputs, cards, typography, etc.) and reuse or extend them instead of duplicating
- Prefer immutable data structures
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Do NOT write comments unless explicitly requested
- **ALWAYS extract business logic from UI into ViewModels** - UI components should only handle presentation, all business logic, data loading, error handling, and state management must be in ViewModels

## File Structure
- `composeApp/src/commonMain/` - Shared code across all platforms
- `composeApp/src/androidMain/` - Android-specific code
- `composeApp/src/iosMain/` - iOS-specific code
- `composeApp/src/wasmJsMain/` - Web-specific code
- `iosApp/` - iOS app entry point

## Build Commands
- Android: `./gradlew :composeApp:assembleDebug`
- Web: `./gradlew :composeApp:wasmJsBrowserDevelopmentRun`
- iOS: Open `iosApp/` in Xcode

## Server Access
- SSH: `ssh root@157.22.252.70`

## Verification Before Completion
- Follow the **verification-before-completion** skill: no claims that work is complete, fixed, or passing without **fresh** command output and exit code
- **Default verification command for this repo:** `./gradlew check` from the repository root (or `:ModuleName:check` only when it fully covers the changed code)
- Read full Gradle output; exit code 0 required before asserting success

## Development Workflow
- No need to run full checks locally - push to CI instead
- CI will run all tests and checks automatically on GitHub Actions
- For quick local verification (optional): `./gradlew :ModuleName:check`
- NEVER run `./gradlew clean` without explicit user permission
- Cache cleaning should only be done when user specifically requests it

## CI failures — how to debug
- **Do not** start by running `./gradlew check`, full multiplatform compiles, or other broad rebuilds just to discover what broke.
- **First** determine which workflow and job failed and read the failure: **GitHub MCP** (if the GitHub server is connected and exposes tools), **GitHub Actions UI**, or the **public REST API** (`GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs?branch=…`, then `/runs/{id}/jobs`) to see job names and conclusions. Job log download via API may require repo permissions; use the web UI or authenticated MCP when logs are 403.
- **Then** reproduce narrowly: e.g. only `./gradlew :Network:check` or the single failing task shown in the log, not the whole tree.

## Architecture Rules - MVVM/MVI Pattern
- **ALWAYS extract ALL business logic from UI into ViewModels** - UI components should ONLY handle presentation
- **UI should ONLY display what comes from ViewModel state** - no business logic, data processing, or state management in UI
- **All state management must be in ViewModel** - use StateFlow/State to expose state to UI
- **All user actions should be sent as Intent to ViewModel** - UI sends Intent, ViewModel processes it and updates state
- **UI should be reactive** - observe ViewModel state and reactively update when state changes
- **No local state in UI** - avoid `remember { mutableStateOf() }` for business logic, use ViewModel state instead
- **Follow MVI pattern** - Model (State), View (UI), Intent (User Actions) - all handled through ViewModel

## Flow-first Approach
- **Prefer Kotlin Flow over one-shot APIs** for any state that can change over time (filters, selection, search results, auth, settings)
- **Repositories expose `Flow` / `StateFlow`** as the source of truth; avoid imperative getters that go stale
- **Derive state with operators** (`map`, `combine`, `flatMapLatest`, `stateIn`) instead of manually copying values between MutableStateFlows
- **ViewModels collect and transform repository flows** into UI state; do not re-fetch or re-sync in ad-hoc callbacks when a flow already exists
- **One-shot `suspend` is OK** only for fire-and-forget actions (submit, delete, navigate side-effects) that do not represent ongoing state
- **UI observes flows reactively** (`collectAsState` / `collectAsStateWithLifecycle`) — no polling or manual refresh loops for flow-backed data

## TDD
- **Write the failing test first**, then the minimal implementation that makes it pass, then refactor
- **Do not add production code without a failing test** that describes the expected behavior
- **Always stage and commit new/updated tests together with the production code** — never leave test files untracked or out of the commit
- **One behavior per test** — name tests by the scenario and expected outcome
- Prefer unit tests in `commonTest` next to the module under change; extend existing test files when the same type is already covered
- Red → Green → Refactor: keep each cycle small; do not implement extra features while making the current test pass
- When fixing a bug: first add a regression test that fails, then fix the code

## Git Workflow Rules
- **NEVER push directly to `main` branch**
- **ALWAYS create feature branches** for new changes
- **ALWAYS use Pull Requests** to merge changes into `main`
- **Workflow**: create branch → make changes → push branch → create PR → merge to main
- **Exception**: Only emergency hotfixes can be pushed directly to main (with immediate PR follow-up)
- **ALWAYS `git add` all new project files** as soon as you create them (new modules, sources, tests, resources, Gradle includes) — do not leave `??` untracked until the user asks
- After creating or renaming files: run `git status` and stage every new path that belongs to the change (exclude secrets, local-only junk, and unrelated dirty files like accidental `.cursorrules` / `gradle.properties` edits unless those were part of the task)
- Staging new files is **not** a commit — still only commit when the user explicitly asks
